Teach pwm-fan about an active-low fan and a supply that gates the fan.

The AYN Odin (SDM845) fan is driven off a PMI8998 LPG channel and is
ACTIVE-LOW: low PWM duty = full speed, high duty = stopped. Neither mainline
pwm-fan (polarity comes only from the pwms specifier) nor leds-qcom-lpg (its
.apply hard-rejects PWM_POLARITY_INVERTED and offers no of_pwm_xlate_with_flags)
can express this in the device tree, so a normal pwm-fan hwmon runs the fan at
~full whenever it thinks it is idling.

Honour three device-tree properties (already present in sdm845-ayn-odin.dts,
matching the jenneron odin tree the node was ported from) instead of silently
ignoring them:

  pwm-inverted             invert the pwm-value -> duty-cycle mapping so a
                           higher hwmon pwm1 value means a faster fan
  fan-supply-off-on-zero   at pwm1=0, cut the fan-supply regulator for a true
                           OFF (on an active-low fan duty 0 == full speed, so
                           holding the line is not enough)
  initial-pwm = <N>        the pwm value to program at probe (default 70, the
                           value the ROCKNIX 0500 boot-fanspeed patch hardcodes);
                           the Odin sets 0 so the fan stays off during boot until
                           armada-powerd takes over

Applies on top of 0500-ROCKNIX-set-boot-fanspeed.patch (which turned the probe
call into set_pwm(ctx, 70)). Devices that do not set these properties are
unaffected: pwm_inverted/fan_off_on_zero default false and initial-pwm falls
back to 70, preserving current behaviour.

diff -rupbN linux.orig/drivers/hwmon/pwm-fan.c linux/drivers/hwmon/pwm-fan.c
--- linux.orig/drivers/hwmon/pwm-fan.c
+++ linux/drivers/hwmon/pwm-fan.c
@@ -65,6 +65,8 @@
 	u64 pwm_duty_cycle_from_stopped;
 	u32 pwm_usec_from_stopped;
 	u8 pwm_shutdown;
+	bool pwm_inverted;
+	bool fan_off_on_zero;
 };
 
 /* This handler assumes self resetting edge triggered interrupt. */
@@ -219,20 +221,26 @@
 		if (update)
 			state->duty_cycle = ctx->pwm_duty_cycle_from_stopped;
 		else
-			state->duty_cycle = DIV_ROUND_UP(pwm * (period - 1), MAX_PWM);
+			state->duty_cycle = ctx->pwm_inverted ?
+				DIV_ROUND_UP((MAX_PWM - pwm) * (period - 1), MAX_PWM) :
+				DIV_ROUND_UP(pwm * (period - 1), MAX_PWM);
 		ret = pwm_apply_might_sleep(ctx->pwm, state);
 		if (ret)
 			return ret;
 		ret = pwm_fan_power_on(ctx);
 		if (!ret && update) {
 			pwm = final_pwm;
-			state->duty_cycle = DIV_ROUND_UP(pwm * (period - 1), MAX_PWM);
+			state->duty_cycle = ctx->pwm_inverted ?
+				DIV_ROUND_UP((MAX_PWM - pwm) * (period - 1), MAX_PWM) :
+				DIV_ROUND_UP(pwm * (period - 1), MAX_PWM);
 			usleep_range(ctx->pwm_usec_from_stopped,
 				     ctx->pwm_usec_from_stopped * 2);
 			ret = pwm_apply_might_sleep(ctx->pwm, state);
 		}
 	} else {
-		ret = pwm_fan_power_off(ctx, false);
+		ret = pwm_fan_power_off(ctx, ctx->fan_off_on_zero);
+		if (!ret && ctx->fan_off_on_zero)
+			ret = pwm_fan_switch_power(ctx, false);
 	}
 	if (!ret)
 		ctx->pwm_value = pwm;
@@ -561,12 +569,17 @@
 		initial_pwm = ctx->pwm_fan_cooling_levels[ctx->pwm_fan_max_state];
 	else
 		initial_pwm = MAX_PWM;
+
+	ctx->pwm_inverted = device_property_read_bool(dev, "pwm-inverted");
+	ctx->fan_off_on_zero = device_property_read_bool(dev, "fan-supply-off-on-zero");
 
 	/*
 	 * Set duty cycle to maximum allowed and enable PWM output as well as
 	 * the regulator. In case of error nothing is changed
 	 */
-	ret = set_pwm(ctx, 70);
+	if (device_property_read_u32(dev, "initial-pwm", &initial_pwm))
+		initial_pwm = 70;
+	ret = set_pwm(ctx, initial_pwm);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(dev, "Failed to configure PWM: %d\n", ret);
 		return ret;
